course: dictionary
lesson: 3874
title: Words with more than one meaning: fray
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which one to use. The word fray can mean loose threads, tense nerves, or a fight. «A frayed or damaged cord should be unplugged by an adult and not used again until it is fixed.» Here frayed means the cord's threads have come loose and it is worn out.
explain: Read the clues. If the sentence is about cloth, rope or cables, fray means the threads are coming loose at the edge. If the sentence is about people's feelings, it can mean tense and annoyed. «Tempers frayed at the dinner table» means everyone became cross and tense.
explain: The fray on its own means a fight, argument or competition. «She decided to join the fray» means she jumped into the argument. Always ask: which meaning fits the sentence? Then swap it in to check that it still makes sense.
fact: Fray means the threads come loose at the edge of cloth or rope, and it starts to wear out.
fact: If someone's nerves or temper fray, they become tense and start to get annoyed.
fact: The fray means a fight, argument or competition.
fact: «Never touch or use a frayed cable with wires showing; tell a grown-up.»
fact: Phrases: «frayed edges», «tempers frayed», «join the fray», «above the fray» (staying out of an argument).
exercise: «The cable's covering was frayed, with wires showing.» What does frayed mean here? | Brand new | *Coming loose and worn at the edge | Bright red | Smooth and shiny
exercise: Dad says, «Tempers frayed at dinner.» What does it mean? | Everyone started to sing | Everyone felt calm and happy | *Everyone became tense and started to get annoyed | Everyone ate very quickly
exercise: «Mia decided to join the fray of the argument.» What is the fray? | *A fight or argument | A small bird that sings | A quiet walk in the park | A kind of bread
test: «A frayed cord should be unplugged by an adult.» Why should a grown-up unplug it? | Because it is a new cable | Because cords like to be cold | Because it is too short | *Because it may be dangerous to use
test: Which phrase means staying out of an argument? | Tempers frayed | *Above the fray | Join the fray | Frayed edges
exam: Sam says fray always means a fight. Is Sam right? | No, it means a small bird | *No, it can also mean loose threads or tense nerves | Yes, it only means a cable | Yes, it always means a fight
